WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.000 --> 00:00:03.800
Hey Crazies.

00:00:03.800 --> 00:00:06.800
Whenever someone starts talking about an expanding
universe

00:00:06.800 --> 00:00:10.040
there’s always at least one person asking
this:

00:00:10.040 --> 00:00:12.300
What is it expanding into?

00:00:12.300 --> 00:00:16.180
OK, we can tackle this, but I’m not sure
you’re going to like the answer.

00:00:16.180 --> 00:00:17.320
Demo time!

00:00:17.320 --> 00:00:20.660
Let’s say you have a balloon with some little
thingies attached.

00:00:20.660 --> 00:00:26.580
These represent galactic super-clusters, which
contain 10s of 1000s of galaxies.

00:00:26.580 --> 00:00:28.680
Yes, super-clusters.

00:00:28.680 --> 00:00:31.439
We don’t see the expansion of the universe
on scales any smaller than that

00:00:31.439 --> 00:00:35.750
because other forces like gravity are strong
enough to hold things together in spite of

00:00:35.750 --> 00:00:36.750
the expansion.

00:00:36.750 --> 00:00:38.560
Stick it to the man, galaxies!!!

00:00:38.560 --> 00:00:40.100
Alright, back to the balloon.

00:00:40.100 --> 00:00:45.100
These represent galactic super-clusters, whereas
the balloon itself represents space.

00:00:45.100 --> 00:00:50.200
If you blow air into the balloon, it expands
carrying the superclusters with it.

00:00:50.200 --> 00:00:55.700
They got farther apart without having to do
any actual moving in space.

00:00:55.700 --> 00:00:56.740
Pretty cool, huh?

00:00:56.740 --> 00:00:58.100
Well, hold up a sec.

00:00:58.100 --> 00:01:00.240
There are a few problems with this demo.

00:01:00.240 --> 00:01:05.200
First, we’re representing our 3D space on
a 2D balloon surface.

00:01:05.200 --> 00:01:07.560
The balloon demo is 1 dimension short.

00:01:07.560 --> 00:01:11.380
It really only represents a slice or cross-section
of the universe,

00:01:11.380 --> 00:01:16.880
but, then again, we drop from 3D to 1D in
spacetime diagrams all the time and nobody

00:01:16.880 --> 00:01:17.800
questions it.

00:01:17.800 --> 00:01:23.100
The second problem is the expanding balloon
has curvature, positive curvature.

00:01:23.100 --> 00:01:26.000
You can see it’s finite and has no edges.

00:01:26.000 --> 00:01:26.780
However!

00:01:26.780 --> 00:01:31.960
We said in the last video that all evidence
suggests we live in a very flat, zero-curvature

00:01:31.960 --> 00:01:35.680
universe
and, if it’s simple, an infinite universe.

00:01:35.680 --> 00:01:39.380
If that’s the case, this balloon is a terrible
model.

00:01:39.380 --> 00:01:42.820
On top of that, the fact that it’s only
a model can be misleading.

00:01:42.820 --> 00:01:46.580
This balloon is in a room full of air and
all sorts of other stuff.

00:01:46.580 --> 00:01:49.020
There’s a space it expands into.

00:01:49.020 --> 00:01:51.000
But none of that applies to the universe.

00:01:51.009 --> 00:01:54.259
Remember, the entire universe is everything.

00:01:54.260 --> 00:01:59.480
Even if the universe were finite like that
balloon… the balloon is all that there is.

00:01:59.480 --> 00:02:05.300
There is no room, no air, no person blowing
that air, just the balloon.

00:02:05.300 --> 00:02:08.980
So if that’s the universe, it’s not expanding
into anything.

00:02:08.980 --> 00:02:13.820
There is nothing outside it, not empty space,
literally nothing.

00:02:13.820 --> 00:02:16.920
Nothingness can be hard to imagine for a lot
of people,

00:02:16.920 --> 00:02:22.280
but your only other option is an infinite
universe and that comes with its own problems.

00:02:22.280 --> 00:02:25.540
You might wonder how the universe could have
gone from microscopic

00:02:25.540 --> 00:02:28.510
to an infinite size in a finite amount of
time.

00:02:28.510 --> 00:02:30.040
Well, it couldn’t have.

00:02:30.040 --> 00:02:35.580
If the universe is infinite, then it always
was for all measurable time.

00:02:35.580 --> 00:02:36.719
What?!?

00:02:36.720 --> 00:02:37.940
No, for real!

00:02:37.940 --> 00:02:43.260
While it is true that everything we can see
was, at one time, the size of microscopic

00:02:43.260 --> 00:02:43.900
speck.

00:02:43.900 --> 00:02:48.940
“Everything we can see” is just our observable
universe, not the entire universe

00:02:48.950 --> 00:02:52.200
and, if the universe is infinite, it solves
our nothingness issue

00:02:52.200 --> 00:02:55.319
because the universe can just expand into
itself.

00:02:55.319 --> 00:03:01.180
As space stretches, we get more and more space,
but, if space is infinite, that’s just more

00:03:01.180 --> 00:03:02.360
and more infinity.

00:03:02.360 --> 00:03:05.680
When you run the clock in reverse, a similar
thing happens.

00:03:05.680 --> 00:03:09.260
As space contracts, you just have less and
less infinity,

00:03:09.260 --> 00:03:12.360
but infinity is infinity, no matter how small.

00:03:12.360 --> 00:03:13.820
Infinity is so weird.

00:03:13.820 --> 00:03:17.400
There’s also no reason to assume that our
observable part is anything special.

00:03:17.400 --> 00:03:22.680
No matter where you are in this infinite universe,
you’ll see an observable part that’s about

00:03:22.680 --> 00:03:25.469
the same size, with you at the center.

00:03:25.469 --> 00:03:30.060
Further into the past, that bubble was a lot
smaller, denser, and hotter,

00:03:30.060 --> 00:03:32.080
but that was true everywhere.

00:03:32.080 --> 00:03:36.920
In an infinite universe, there is no edge,
there is no center

00:03:36.920 --> 00:03:39.540
and the big bang happened everywhere.

00:03:39.540 --> 00:03:40.829
Which kinda bothers me a little.

00:03:40.829 --> 00:03:43.370
If the universe is infinite and nowhere is
special,

00:03:43.370 --> 00:03:47.340
does that means there are also an infinity
of me out there somewhere beyond the cosmic

00:03:47.340 --> 00:03:48.080
horizon?

00:03:48.080 --> 00:03:51.760
Am I wasting my time cloning when I should
just invent FTL travel?

00:03:51.760 --> 00:03:53.960
I didn't realize I was going to get to be on camera.

00:03:53.960 --> 00:03:57.280
If there are infinite possibilities, why does
it all look the same?

00:03:57.280 --> 00:03:58.480
What do you think?

00:03:58.480 --> 00:03:59.820
Are there infinite copies of all of us?

00:03:59.820 --> 00:04:01.080
Let me know in the comments.

00:04:01.080 --> 00:04:02.620
Thanks for liking and sharing this video.

00:04:02.620 --> 00:04:04.920
Don’t forget to subscribe if you'd like to
keep up with us.

00:04:04.920 --> 00:04:08.400
And until next time, remember, it’s OK to
be a little crazy.

